  "query_preview": "..\n\nInfluence of high pressure wanes over the course of the morning as \nit shifts into Quebec. As this occurs, the next weather maker takes \nshape over the Midwest as southern/northern stream waves phase \nresulting in developing low pressure near the upper-Mississippi. \nAssociated surface warm front lifts through the Ohio Valley today \nwith the elevated portions of the frontal slope lifting over \nsouthern lower MI. Actual activation of the slope however is delayed \nuntil late afternoon (~20-21Z) when lead vorticity/theta-e finally \nreaches the area favoring a greater coverage of moderate showers \nover the northern half of SE MI (north of M-59) through this \nevening. Secondary low feature along the occluding front over \nnorthern IN/OH remains present in higher res model output resulting \nin the surface warm front being confined on or just south of the \nOhio border. An interesting potential outcome with instability being \nheld south of the area and the main low track trending a touch \nfurther north towards the Saginaw Valley, is a corridor within the \nsouthern portions of the CWA (ranging somewhere from the M-59 to I-\n94 corridors) of notably less shower development due to falling in \nbetween the areas of stronger forcing. This is scenario can be seen \nin the most extreme case in the NAM but a flavor of which is present \nin the ARW/HRRR. Areawide, general QPF is expected to range between \n0.25-0.5\" (highest north) though if the outcome above is realized, \nsome locales could see only a tenth or so of rainfall. \n\nLow departs into southern Ontario by Friday morning tapering off the \nmajority of activity over SE MI. Parent mid-upper trough slides \nacross lower MI during the day bringing significantly colder air \naloft as 500mb temps fall some 20 degrees into the mid -20's C. This \nongoing cold advection within the diurnal window steepens lapse \nrates as passing trough influence supports modest upper divergence. \nWhile the bulk of moisture is east of the region by this point, \nmoisture wrapping around the mature low as well as lake flux should \nresult in pretty good coverage (scattered to numerous) convective \nshowers by midday with peak coverage in the afternoon aided by a \nsecondary cold front dropping through the state. This front marks \nthe lead edge of the more robust CAA as 850mb temps fall from \nroughly +6C to -3C by evening. Lake surface temps currently hover in \nthe mid teens C so more than enough delta T to spark lake effect \nrain showers. Winds turn north post-front favoring these bands to \nwork across the Thumb and down into Metro Detroit- particularly the \neastern half. \n\nFor winds Friday, the gradient weakens following the low/initial \ncold front with lower level column wind within the mixed layer \nlooking to support peak NW gusts up around 25kts (~30mph). This \nchanges with the arrival of the secondary cold front late afternoon-\nevening, coincident with the passage of the trough axis, leading to \na tightening gradient and column wind fields increasing towards 30-\n35kts. Forecast soundings are favorable for sufficient mixing depths \nthrough the evening to tap into this flow to push peak N gusts to \n35mph with 40mph likely along the Lake Huron shoreline. \n\nGusty winds and lake effect wind down Friday night as surface high \npressure over the Midwest beings to expand into the central Great \nLakes. Overnight lows bottom out in the lower 30s with sub-freezing \ntemperatures expected in areas north of I-69 though areas on the \nglacial moraine could also see temps drop below 32F. Below normal \ntemperatures in place for Saturday as continuing north wind leads to \nhighs struggling to reach the mid 50s. Surface ridge axis eventually \ncrosses Saturday night as central CONUS upper ridging expands \noverhead setting up southerly, warmer return flow for the new week. \nRidging influence holds through at least the first half of the week, \nif not longer, maintaining an extended period of dry weather and \nabove normal temperatures with highs in the mid 60s.",
